  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "What is the first term that is less than $0.000\\,4$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n128,\\,16,\\,2,\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that is less than $0.000\\,4$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term.",
  "worked_solution": "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=128$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{16}{128}=\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =128{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,4.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.000\\,4 \\\\ 128{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{n-1} & <0.000\\,4 \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{8})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{0.000\\,4}{128} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,003\\,125) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{8}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,003\\,125) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,003\\,125)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{8})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,003\\,125)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{8})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{8})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{8})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,003\\,125)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{8})}+1≈7.096.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $8$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,4.$",
